# Backlog · feature — how to specify a new capability

A **feature** item is a new user-facing capability we want, stated as a **problem and why**,
never as a solution. `/intent` turns it into one or more PRDs (the buildable, runner-verified
form); it may serve a bet, but it carries **no metric of its own** — the metric lives on the
bet.

Write it from the user's side. The reader should understand *who is stuck, and why this
matters*, without being told how to build it.

## What a good feature item says
- **As a user…** — who needs this and the job they're trying to do.
- **Why it matters** — the problem today and the cost of leaving it. If it serves a bet, name
  the indicator it's meant to help move (as rationale, not a metric on the item).
- **Definition of done — at outcome altitude** — what's true for the user when this is done.
  Not endpoints, schemas, or components — that's the PRD/runner's altitude.
- **Out of scope / non-goals** *(optional)* — what this is deliberately not.

## Skeleton
```markdown
---
type: feature
status: backlog
bet: "[[<bet-slug>]]"      # optional
repos: [<repo>]
created: <YYYY-MM-DD>
prd:
---

# <Feature title>

## As a user
As a <role>, I want <capability> so that <outcome>.

## Why it matters
<The problem today, who it hurts, the cost of inaction. If it serves [[<bet-slug>]], note the
indicator it's meant to help move — as rationale, never a check.>

## Definition of done (outcome altitude)
- <what's true for the user when this is done>
- <observable, solution-agnostic>

## Out of scope (optional)
- <explicit non-goals>
```

- **Never name the solution** here (no endpoints, tables, components, libraries). If you catch
  yourself designing, stop — that belongs in the PRD.
- `/intent` sets `status: prd-drafted` and fills `prd:` when it files the PRD against this
  item. A feature may become **several** PRDs — that's fine; `/intent` slices lazily.
